# TypeScript and Node.js

Prefer explicit, maintainable TypeScript. Do not use `any`. Use `unknown` at untrusted boundaries, then narrow.

## Naming

- Classes, types, enums: PascalCase
- Variables, functions, methods: camelCase
- Files and directories: kebab-case
- Constants and env keys: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE

## Types

- Prefer `import type` when a symbol is type-only.
- Mark immutable fields `readonly`.
- Prefer `z.infer<typeof schema>` over hand-written DTO types when Zod already describes the shape.
- Do not use `@ts-ignore`. Prefer `@ts-expect-error` with a one-line reason only when unavoidable.

## Functions and errors

- Name functions with a verb and a noun (`getOrderById`).
- Prefer small pure functions in the domain; keep I/O in application or infrastructure.
- Never swallow errors. Log with context, then throw a typed error with `{ cause }`.

```typescript
// ❌
try {
  await save(order);
} catch (e) {}

// ✅
try {
  await save(order);
} catch (error) {
  logger.error('Failed to save order', { orderId: order.id, error });
  throw new OrderPersistenceError('Unable to save order', { cause: error });
}
```

## Node.js

- Treat process input (HTTP, queues, env, files) as untrusted. Validate with Zod before use.
- Prefer `Promise.all` for independent async work. Do not await in a loop unless order or rate limits require it.
- Keep secrets in env, never in source. Validate env with a Zod schema at startup.
